esword4linux for Debian and Ubuntu
===================================

Installation layout
-------------------

  /usr/bin/esword4linux
      Launcher for the private runtime.

  /opt/esword4linux/app/esword4linux
      Application Python package and data (books.yaml, i18n, assets).

  /opt/esword4linux/python
      Private PySide6-Essentials, shiboken6, Qt 6 and pure-Python PyYAML.

  /usr/share/applications/esword4linux.desktop
  /usr/share/icons/hicolor/*/apps/esword4linux.png

The package does not install anything into the system Python site-packages and
does not depend on the distribution Qt libraries at runtime.

No qt.conf is installed. The PySide6 extension modules carry an RPATH relative
to their location ($ORIGIN/Qt/lib and $ORIGIN/../shiboken6), and the launcher
sets QT_PLUGIN_PATH to the private plugin directory. This was verified by
loading libqxcb.so with QT_DEBUG_PLUGINS=1; adding qt.conf would duplicate a
resolution path Qt already performs correctly.

User data
---------

  ~/.config/esword4linux/settings.json
      Preferences and the selected modules folder.

  ~/.cache/esword4linux/
      Regenerable catalog and FTS5 index.

Removing or upgrading the package does not delete these paths and never
modifies the user's e-Sword modules folder.

X11 and Wayland
---------------

The private Qt runtime includes both the xcb and Wayland platform plugins. The
launcher does not force QT_QPA_PLATFORM; Qt chooses according to the session.
On a Wayland session that also has XWayland, users who want the native Wayland
backend can run:

    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland esword4linux

To use X11 explicitly:

    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb esword4linux

The build host was Linux Mint 22.3 (Ubuntu 24.04/noble base) with an X11
session. Wayland was validated with Weston 13 headless/kiosk (platform plugin
and window process); a full desktop compositor session and a clean Ubuntu
24.04 VM were not available. See the validation matrix in
/usr/share/doc/esword4linux/INSTALL.md.

Runtime override for testing
----------------------------

The launcher honors ESWORD4LINUX_ROOT (default /opt/esword4linux) and
ESWORD4LINUX_PYTHON (default /usr/bin/python3). These are intended for
portable builds and QA; normal installations should not set them.

Rebuilding
----------

From a source checkout:

    packaging/build-deb.sh

The build uses pinned wheels with SHA-256 hashes. See
/opt/esword4linux/RUNTIME-MANIFEST.yaml for the exact runtime
inventory and the rationale for retained and excluded Qt components.

Debian source package
---------------------

The `3.0 (quilt)` source package embeds the three pinned wheels in the
upstream tarball, because buildds have no PyPI access:

    make deb-src-verify

`packaging/build_source.py` creates the deterministic
`<source>_<version>.orig.tar.xz`, excludes `debian/` from it, validates the
wheel hashes and the source checksums, and can rebuild the binary offline.
Wheels are never tracked in Git. This packaging is intended for a Launchpad
PPA; prebuilt wheels are not directly suitable for the Debian/Ubuntu main
archives.

Licensing and library replacement
---------------------------------

The installed runtime ships the Qt/PySide6 license texts and Qt third-party
attribution under `/usr/share/doc/esword4linux/licenses/`. Read
`/usr/share/doc/esword4linux/LGPL-COMPLIANCE.md` for the covered components,
the source archives and the tested procedure for replacing private Qt/PySide6
libraries.

